# Cumulus - the spectral pad machine, voiced against the debug build.
#
# Every patch here is a *spectrum*, not a signal chain. Each harmonic goes in
# as a band of lines with random phases rather than as one line, the whole
# thing is inverse transformed into a table a second and a half long, and a
# voice reads it. That is why the patches are grouped below by what the
# spectrum is rather than by what the filter does.
#
# **The four knobs that make families rather than shades:**
#
#   `stretch`   puts partial n at n^(1+stretch). Off zero it walks the same
#               patch from choir to piano to bell to gong, and below zero it
#               does something nothing acoustic does - crowds the partials
#               *towards* the fundamental.
#   `bandwidth` is how wide each band is, in cents. Narrow keeps every partial
#               audible on its own; wide stops being a chorus and becomes
#               weather. Crossed with stretch, that is most of this bank.
#   `odd`       hollows the series out. At 1.0 it is a tube stopped at one end.
#   `vowel`     is three formants in the profile, which is a voice made out of
#               the spectrum instead of out of a filter.
#
# **And the second spectrum.** Every `b`-prefixed value is the far end of a
# morph, written as a difference from the near end, so one patch can be dark
# and narrow at the bottom of the keyboard and bright and smeared at the top
# (`morphkey`), or walk between the two over half a minute (`lfo1morph`).
# Four tables are built along that path and crossfaded; they share their
# random phases, which is the trick that stops two of them cancelling.
#
# **Levels.** The bank is levelled to the house line by `volume`. A pad is
# judged on what happens while it is held, so the measurements are taken from
# a phrase that holds.
#
# Values are in each parameter's own units; `audition params Cumulus` lists
# them. A patch names only what it changes.

machine Cumulus
# Four demos, because a bell is not a pad and neither is a drone:
#   pad   - four chords, each held four seconds, overlapping
#   bell  - struck and left, with room for the tail
#   keys  - a root underneath and chords on the off beats
#   drone - one chord held twenty seconds, notes joining and leaving it,
#           because the drift and the morph are slower than any other phrase
role    pad

patch Init
# High and thin, with the bands wide enough that no partial is a line
# any more. Nothing down low at all - this is the one that goes over
# something else.
patch Cirrus note=72 range=48..96 family=pad
  partials       56
  tilt           -10 dB/oct
  bandwidth      35 cents
  bbandwidth     90 cents
  bwscale        1.1
  detune         7 cents
  width          0.7
  cutoff         11000 Hz
  ampattack      0.8 s
  amprelease     2.2 s
  drift          8 cents
  driftrate      0.09 Hz
  volume         1.03

# The other half of the sky: low, slow and enormous. Four seconds to
# arrive and five to go, which is not an effect - it is the whole
# difference between a pad and a chord.
patch Stratus note=52 range=36..79 family=pad
  partials       72
  tilt           -15 dB/oct
  bandwidth      95 cents
  bbandwidth     -40 cents
  bwscale        1.3
  detune         12 cents
  drift          12 cents
  driftrate      0.05 Hz
  width          0.85
  cutoff         5200 Hz
  ampattack      3.6 s
  ampdecay       4 s
  ampsustain     0.9
  amprelease     5 s
  spread         #2
  volume         1.37

# Thick through the middle where most music actually sits, and moving
# enough that a held chord is never quite the same twice.
patch Nimbus note=60 range=36..84 family=pad
  partials       64
  tilt           -11 dB/oct
  bandwidth      55 cents
  bbandwidth     60 cents
  bwscale        1.15
  detune         10 cents
  drift          16 cents
  driftrate      0.13 Hz
  width          0.75
  cutoff         7600 Hz
  ampattack      1.2 s
  amprelease     2.8 s
  spread         #2
  volume         1.36

# The one to reach for without reading anything: soft, mid, no tricks.
# Every bank needs one patch that just plays chords.
patch "Warm Bed" note=60 range=36..84 family=pad
  partials       40
  tilt           -13 dB/oct
  bandwidth      28 cents
  bbandwidth     35 cents
  detune         8 cents
  drift          7 cents
  width          0.6
  cutoff         6400 Hz
  ampattack      0.55 s
  amprelease     1.8 s
  volume         0.87

# Bands six semitones wide, which stops being a chorus and becomes
# weather. The second spectrum is *narrower*, so the morph pulls it back
# into focus rather than opening it further.
patch "Deep Wash" note=48 range=33..72 family=pad
  partials       64
  tilt           -16 dB/oct
  bandwidth      180 cents
  bbandwidth     -120 cents
  bwscale        1.4
  detune         14 cents
  drift          14 cents
  driftrate      0.07 Hz
  width          0.9
  cutoff         6000 Hz
  ampattack      2.5 s
  amprelease     5 s
  spread         #2
  volume         1.12

# Narrow bands and a high tilt: the partials stay separate, so it rings
# rather than washes. Bright without being hard.
patch Halo note=72 range=48..96 family=pad
  partials       48
  tilt           -6 dB/oct
  bandwidth      12 cents
  bbandwidth     40 cents
  detune         5 cents
  drift          5 cents
  driftrate      0.2 Hz
  width          0.8
  cutoff         15000 Hz
  ampattack      0.45 s
  amprelease     2.6 s
  shimmer        0.25
  shimmerint     #1
  volume         0.86

# Quiet and filtered back, made to sit under something. A pad that
# insists on being heard is not a pad.
patch Veil note=60 range=36..84 family=pad
  partials       52
  tilt           -18 dB/oct
  bandwidth      70 cents
  bbandwidth     30 cents
  bwscale        1.2
  detune         9 cents
  drift          10 cents
  width          0.7
  cutoff         3200 Hz
  resonance      0.15
  ampattack      1.8 s
  amprelease     3.4 s
  volume         1.01

# Three formants, interpolated, on a spectrum wide enough to be a
# section rather than a person.
patch Choir note=60 range=43..84 family=voice
  partials       44
  tilt           -8 dB/oct
  bandwidth      22 cents
  bbandwidth     40 cents
  vowel          0.25
  vowelamount    0.85
  bvowel         0.3
  detune         9 cents
  drift          9 cents
  driftrate      0.1 Hz
  width          0.8
  cutoff         8000 Hz
  morphkey       0.3
  spread         #2
  ampattack      0.5 s
  amprelease     2 s
  volume         1.00

# The dark vowel with the formants well up. All of the character is in
# three bumps in the spectrum and none of it in a filter.
patch Ooh note=55 range=40..79 family=voice
  partials       36
  tilt           -12 dB/oct
  bandwidth      18 cents
  bbandwidth     25 cents
  vowel          0.92
  vowelamount    1
  bvowel         -0.25
  detune         7 cents
  drift          8 cents
  width          0.7
  cutoff         5000 Hz
  ampattack      0.7 s
  amprelease     2.2 s
  volume         0.79

# The open one, brighter and further forward. Against Ooh this is the
# same machine with one knob moved, which is the point of a formant.
patch Ahh note=62 range=45..84 family=voice
  partials       48
  tilt           -9 dB/oct
  bandwidth      20 cents
  bbandwidth     45 cents
  vowel          0.05
  vowelamount    0.95
  bvowel         0.4
  detune         8 cents
  drift          9 cents
  width          0.75
  cutoff         9500 Hz
  ampattack      0.55 s
  amprelease     2.1 s
  volume         1.13

# A voice with the bands opened until the pitch is only just there.
# Quiet, close, and more air than note.
patch "Whisper Choir" note=67 range=48..88 family=voice
  partials       60
  tilt           -14 dB/oct
  bandwidth      210 cents
  bbandwidth     90 cents
  bwscale        1.5
  vowel          0.5
  vowelamount    0.7
  detune         13 cents
  drift          18 cents
  driftrate      0.16 Hz
  width          1
  cutoff         11000 Hz
  ampattack      1.4 s
  amprelease     2.8 s
  volume         1.10

# One vowel at the bottom of the keyboard and another at the top, with
# the morph on key tracking. The same chord says two different things
# depending on where you play it, which no sampled choir does.
patch "Vowel Morph" note=60 range=43..84 family=voice role=drone
  partials       46
  tilt           -9 dB/oct
  bandwidth      24 cents
  bbandwidth     20 cents
  vowel          0.05
  vowelamount    0.9
  bvowel         0.85
  btilt          -4 dB/oct
  morphkey       1
  detune         8 cents
  drift          10 cents
  width          0.8
  cutoff         8600 Hz
  ampattack      0.8 s
  amprelease     2.6 s
  volume         1.03

# Partial n at n^1.032, which is what makes a bell a bell: the overtones
# are sharp of harmonic and get sharper as they climb. Narrow bands so
# each one is audible on its own.
patch "Bell Cloud" note=72 range=48..91 family=bell role=bell
  partials       28
  tilt           -6 dB/oct
  bandwidth      10 cents
  bbandwidth     120 cents
  stretch        0.032
  bstretch       0.02
  comb           0.35
  combperiod     4
  shimmer        0.2
  spread         #0
  cutoff         14000 Hz
  filterenv      0.3
  ampattack      0.004 s
  ampdecay       4 s
  ampsustain     0.25
  amprelease     5 s
  volume         1.17

# A long tube rather than a cast bell: fewer partials, less stretch, and
# a decay you can wait out.
patch Tubular note=67 range=48..88 family=bell role=bell
  partials       18
  tilt           -4 dB/oct
  bandwidth      7 cents
  bbandwidth     60 cents
  stretch        0.018
  odd            0.72
  detune         3 cents
  drift          4 cents
  cutoff         12000 Hz
  ampattack      0.003 s
  ampdecay       6 s
  ampsustain     0.15
  amprelease     7 s
  volume         0.99

# High, thin and lit from above: the shimmer reads the same table two
# octaves up, so the sparkle is made of the sound itself rather than of
# a pitch shifter after it.
patch "Glass Rain" note=79 range=55..100 family=bell role=bell
  partials       40
  tilt           -12 dB/oct
  bandwidth      28 cents
  bbandwidth     150 cents
  odd            0.75
  bodd           -0.5
  btilt          8 dB/oct
  shimmer        0.55
  shimmerint     #3
  detune         6 cents
  width          0.9
  cutoff         16000 Hz
  lfo1morph      0.8
  lfo1rate       0.08 Hz
  ampattack      0.3 s
  amprelease     3 s
  volume         0.62

# Stretch and a deep scallop together, hit hard. The comb is what makes
# it sound like a plate with a shape rather than a tone with an attack.
patch "Struck Metal" note=64 range=45..88 family=bell role=bell
  partials       34
  tilt           -5 dB/oct
  bandwidth      9 cents
  bbandwidth     80 cents
  stretch        0.04
  comb           0.65
  combperiod     2.5
  bcomb          -0.4
  cutoff         15000 Hz
  filterenv      0.45
  filtdecay      1.2 s
  ampattack      0.002 s
  ampdecay       2.6 s
  ampsustain     0.18
  amprelease     3.2 s
  volume         0.98

# A big one, struck once and left. The stretch is wide enough that the
# top of it is no longer in the same key as the bottom, which is what a
# large bell actually does.
patch Temple note=48 range=36..72 family=bell role=bell
  partials       44
  tilt           -7 dB/oct
  bandwidth      22 cents
  bbandwidth     160 cents
  stretch        0.048
  bstretch       0.01
  comb           0.25
  combperiod     5
  detune         11 cents
  drift          9 cents
  driftrate      0.04 Hz
  width          0.9
  cutoff         9000 Hz
  ampattack      0.01 s
  ampdecay       7 s
  ampsustain     0.3
  amprelease     9 s
  volume         1.16

# Small, bright and gone almost at once. Few partials, narrow bands, and
# a decay measured in fractions of a second.
patch "Music Box" note=84 range=60..103 family=bell role=bell
  partials       14
  tilt           -3 dB/oct
  bandwidth      5 cents
  bbandwidth     30 cents
  stretch        0.028
  odd            0.65
  detune         2 cents
  cutoff         17000 Hz
  ampattack      0.002 s
  ampdecay       0.85 s
  ampsustain     0.05
  amprelease     1.1 s
  volume         1.27

# Stretch at the top of its travel with the bands wide open: the
# partials are neither harmonic nor separable, which is the sound of a
# large sheet of metal deciding what note it is.
patch Gong note=45 range=33..72 family=gong role=bell
  partials       80
  tilt           -8 dB/oct
  bandwidth      120 cents
  bbandwidth     200 cents
  bwscale        1.6
  stretch        0.055
  bstretch       -0.02
  detune         16 cents
  drift          20 cents
  driftrate      0.06 Hz
  width          1
  cutoff         10000 Hz
  ampattack      0.02 s
  ampdecay       8 s
  ampsustain     0.35
  amprelease     10 s
  spread         #2
  volume         1.22

# The same idea with a scallop cut through it, so it rings in bands
# instead of everywhere at once.
patch "Sheet Metal" note=52 range=36..79 family=gong role=bell
  partials       66
  tilt           -6 dB/oct
  bandwidth      60 cents
  bbandwidth     -30 cents
  stretch        0.042
  comb           0.8
  combperiod     2
  bcomb          -0.6
  detune         13 cents
  drift          15 cents
  width          0.95
  cutoff         13000 Hz
  ampattack      0.008 s
  ampdecay       4.5 s
  ampsustain     0.25
  amprelease     5.5 s
  volume         1.18

# Stretch below zero, which nothing acoustic does: the partials crowd
# *towards* the fundamental instead of away from it, and the result
# sounds like a room rather than an instrument.
patch Cavern note=45 range=33..72 family=gong
  partials       70
  tilt           -14 dB/oct
  bandwidth      140 cents
  bbandwidth     60 cents
  bwscale        1.35
  stretch        -0.018
  bstretch       0.01
  detune         15 cents
  drift          18 cents
  driftrate      0.05 Hz
  width          1
  cutoff         4800 Hz
  ampattack      2.8 s
  amprelease     6 s
  spread         #2
  volume         1.03

# The whole cloud, given a hard attack and a short decay. A pad machine
# playing something that is not a pad, which the envelope alone decides.
patch "Spectral Pluck" note=60 range=40..84 family=pluck role=keys
  partials       42
  tilt           -10 dB/oct
  bandwidth      16 cents
  bbandwidth     70 cents
  stretch        0.006
  detune         6 cents
  drift          4 cents
  cutoff         11000 Hz
  filterenv      0.5
  filtdecay      0.6 s
  filterkey      0.5
  ampattack      0.002 s
  ampdecay       1.1 s
  ampsustain     0
  amprelease     0.9 s
  volume         1.25

# Softer at the front and longer at the back, with the bands narrow
# enough to keep the pitch clear under a fast passage.
patch "Harp Cloud" note=64 range=43..88 family=pluck role=keys
  partials       36
  tilt           -11 dB/oct
  bandwidth      11 cents
  bbandwidth     45 cents
  odd            0.58
  detune         4 cents
  drift          3 cents
  width          0.7
  cutoff         10000 Hz
  ampattack      0.006 s
  ampdecay       2.2 s
  ampsustain     0.08
  amprelease     2 s
  volume         1.10

# Struck strings with a little stretch on them and a lot of top. The
# unison spread is what does the ringing, not a reverb.
patch Cimbalom note=64 range=45..88 family=pluck role=keys
  partials       52
  tilt           -7 dB/oct
  bandwidth      14 cents
  bbandwidth     55 cents
  stretch        0.012
  detune         14 cents
  spread         #2
  spreadwidth    0.9
  cutoff         14000 Hz
  filterenv      0.35
  ampattack      0.002 s
  ampdecay       1.8 s
  ampsustain     0.1
  amprelease     2.4 s
  volume         1.13

# Stretch for the string and a comb for whatever has been laid across
# it. Every note has a different set of partials missing because the
# scallop is fixed in the spectrum while the note moves under it.
patch "Prepared Piano" note=55 range=36..84 family=pluck role=keys
  partials       48
  tilt           -9 dB/oct
  bandwidth      13 cents
  bbandwidth     90 cents
  stretch        0.02
  comb           0.55
  combperiod     3.5
  bcomb          0.4
  detune         7 cents
  cutoff         12000 Hz
  ampattack      0.003 s
  ampdecay       2.4 s
  ampsustain     0.12
  amprelease     2.6 s
  volume         1.50

# Odd partials only, which is a tube stopped at one end - and the
# second spectrum puts the even ones back, so the morph opens it from
# a clarinet into something whole.
patch Hollow note=60 range=36..84 family=hollow
  partials       40
  tilt           -11 dB/oct
  bandwidth      30 cents
  bbandwidth     70 cents
  odd            1
  bodd           -0.6
  comb           0.55
  bcomb          -0.5
  detune         5 cents
  cutoff         7000 Hz
  ampattack      0.9 s
  amprelease     2.4 s
  volume         0.86

# Odd-heavy with a vowel over it: reedy in both senses, and neither of
# them is a filter.
patch "Reed Bed" note=57 range=40..81 family=hollow
  partials       38
  tilt           -10 dB/oct
  bandwidth      24 cents
  bbandwidth     50 cents
  odd            0.88
  vowel          0.35
  vowelamount    0.6
  bodd           -0.3
  detune         9 cents
  drift          11 cents
  width          0.75
  cutoff         6800 Hz
  ampattack      0.6 s
  amprelease     2 s
  volume         1.31

# Narrow bands, odd partials, almost no drift: as close as this machine
# gets to standing still, which against everything else in the bank is
# its own kind of effect.
patch "Pipe Cloud" note=64 range=43..88 family=hollow
  partials       30
  tilt           -9 dB/oct
  bandwidth      6 cents
  bbandwidth     20 cents
  odd            0.92
  detune         2 cents
  drift          2 cents
  driftrate      0.05 Hz
  width          0.5
  cutoff         9000 Hz
  ampattack      0.25 s
  amprelease     1.2 s
  volume         0.86

# A scallop three partials wide cut through a plain spectrum. It is a
# phaser's shape without a phaser's motion - the notches are *in the
# sound*, so they do not move when the note does.
patch "Comb Pad" note=60 range=36..84 family=comb
  partials       56
  tilt           -10 dB/oct
  bandwidth      40 cents
  bbandwidth     40 cents
  comb           0.75
  combperiod     3
  bcomb          -0.7
  detune         9 cents
  drift          9 cents
  width          0.8
  cutoff         8800 Hz
  ampattack      1 s
  amprelease     2.6 s
  volume         1.25

# And this is what happens when the notches *do* move: LFO1 walks the
# morph from a deep comb to its opposite, so the peaks and the troughs
# trade places over half a minute.
patch "Phase Cloud" note=60 range=36..84 family=comb role=drone
  partials       60
  tilt           -11 dB/oct
  bandwidth      45 cents
  bbandwidth     20 cents
  comb           0.85
  combperiod     2.5
  bcomb          -1
  btilt          4 dB/oct
  lfo1morph      1
  lfo1rate       0.035 Hz
  detune         10 cents
  drift          12 cents
  width          0.9
  cutoff         9500 Hz
  ampattack      1.6 s
  amprelease     3.6 s
  volume         1.01

# A long-period comb - twelve partials between notches - which reads as
# a formant rather than as a phaser.
patch Scallop note=64 range=43..88 family=comb
  partials       64
  tilt           -8 dB/oct
  bandwidth      26 cents
  bbandwidth     60 cents
  comb           0.6
  combperiod     11
  bcomb          0.3
  detune         7 cents
  drift          7 cents
  width          0.75
  cutoff         10500 Hz
  ampattack      0.7 s
  amprelease     2.2 s
  volume         1.05

# A second reader an octave up, out of the same table. Not a pitch
# shifter and not a delay - the octave is the same cloud, so it drifts
# with it instead of against it.
patch "Shimmer Pad" note=62 range=43..88 family=shimmer
  partials       50
  tilt           -12 dB/oct
  bandwidth      32 cents
  bbandwidth     50 cents
  shimmer        0.6
  shimmerint     #1
  detune         8 cents
  drift          10 cents
  width          0.85
  cutoff         12000 Hz
  ampattack      1.1 s
  amprelease     3.2 s
  volume         1.09

# The same trick at a fifth, which does not fuse the way an octave does
# - you hear two things, and the chord gets an extra note it was never
# given.
patch "Fifth Up" note=62 range=43..88 family=shimmer
  partials       46
  tilt           -10 dB/oct
  bandwidth      26 cents
  bbandwidth     45 cents
  shimmer        0.5
  shimmerint     #0
  detune         7 cents
  drift          8 cents
  width          0.8
  cutoff         11000 Hz
  ampattack      0.9 s
  amprelease     2.8 s
  volume         0.96

# Two octaves up and quiet, which is how you make a low pad sound large
# without making it bright.
patch "Two Octaves" note=57 range=40..84 family=shimmer
  partials       58
  tilt           -15 dB/oct
  bandwidth      60 cents
  bbandwidth     40 cents
  shimmer        0.4
  shimmerint     #3
  detune         11 cents
  drift          13 cents
  driftrate      0.08 Hz
  width          0.9
  cutoff         6500 Hz
  ampattack      1.8 s
  amprelease     4 s
  volume         0.85

# Dark and narrow at the bottom of the keyboard, bright and smeared at
# the top, on one patch. Key tracking drives the morph, so the left hand
# and the right hand are playing two different instruments.
patch "Morph Key" note=60 range=36..88 family=morph role=drone
  partials       64
  tilt           -16 dB/oct
  bandwidth      16 cents
  bbandwidth     180 cents
  btilt          10 dB/oct
  bodd           -0.3
  morphkey       1
  detune         9 cents
  drift          10 cents
  width          0.85
  cutoff         9000 Hz
  filterkey      0.6
  ampattack      1 s
  amprelease     3 s
  volume         0.85

# Thirty seconds from one spectrum to the other and back. Slower than
# any phrase will show you, which is deliberate: it is meant to be
# still moving when the piece ends.
patch "Slow Turn" note=60 range=36..84 family=morph role=drone
  partials       68
  tilt           -12 dB/oct
  bandwidth      50 cents
  bbandwidth     120 cents
  bstretch       0.014
  btilt          6 dB/oct
  bodd           -0.4
  lfo1morph      1
  lfo1rate       0.022 Hz
  detune         11 cents
  drift          14 cents
  driftrate      0.045 Hz
  width          0.9
  cutoff         8200 Hz
  ampattack      2.2 s
  amprelease     4.5 s
  spread         #2
  volume         1.09

# Two slow things at rates that do not divide into each other - the
# morph on one, the filter on the other - so it never arrives at the
# same place twice inside a piece.
patch Breathing note=60 range=36..84 family=morph role=drone
  partials       60
  tilt           -13 dB/oct
  bandwidth      70 cents
  bbandwidth     -30 cents
  bwscale        1.25
  btilt          -5 dB/oct
  lfo1morph      0.85
  lfo1rate       0.037 Hz
  lfo2wave       #0
  lfo2rate       0.059 Hz
  lfo2cutoff     0.5
  detune         12 cents
  drift          16 cents
  driftrate      0.06 Hz
  width          0.95
  cutoff         5600 Hz
  resonance      0.2
  ampattack      2.6 s
  amprelease     5.2 s
  volume         0.87

# Built to be held. The drift is wide, the rate is slow, the scatter is
# full, and every voice starts somewhere else in a table a second and a
# half long - so a chord is never the same cloud twice and never
# settles.
patch "Drone Field" note=45 range=33..72 family=morph role=drone
  partials       88
  tilt           -14 dB/oct
  bandwidth      110 cents
  bbandwidth     70 cents
  bwscale        1.45
  bstretch       0.008
  btilt          3 dB/oct
  lfo1morph      0.7
  lfo1rate       0.028 Hz
  scatter        1
  detune         18 cents
  drift          26 cents
  driftrate      0.04 Hz
  width          1
  spread         #2
  spreadwidth    1
  cutoff         7000 Hz
  ampattack      3.4 s
  amprelease     7 s
  volume         1.25

# Low, narrow and almost nothing above the fifth partial. A pad that
# occupies the bottom of a mix without any of it being a sine.
patch "Sub Cloud" note=40 range=28..60 family=dark
  partials       12
